A||||6||William Federle||Duke splash shields||||1||
Z||000000||William Federle||05-21-2001||07:25 PM||||Does anyone know if there were originally splash shields to prevent water from splashing up onto the starter on the 1984 duke?<P>I have a continuing problem when driving in <BR>heavy rain. The volt-meter reading goes down to about 10 or 11 volts and the belt eventually screeches - then the voltage goes back up. If I drive slower (around 60 MPH) then this happens less frequently.<P>I tried spraying several coats of this stuff that they sell to spray on battery posts to protect them from corrosion - in an attempt to insulate the electrical connections on the starter and solenoid but it doesn't seem to help.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 05-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Raydar||05-21-2001||10:19 PM||||I think the reason your voltmeter is dropping is because water is getting on the belt and allowing it to slip. When it slips the alternator output drops. Mine does the same thing.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000002||William Federle||05-22-2001||01:21 PM||||Thanks for the reply. Do you know if there is a splash shield that is supposed to protect the alternator belt?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||05-22-2001||01:39 PM||||I don't think there is any spash shields for the belts.  You should check the ension of the belts and tighten as required.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000004||HummelHund||05-22-2001||02:23 PM||||My dad's Jetta GLI used to screech like a banshee everytime it rained. A water shield is just something 1980's car designers seem to have overlooked. <BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6 (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>'84 Sport Coupe ($150 running car)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Topknot||05-22-2001||03:36 PM||||Even if it didn't come with on why not make one!  I mean a few dollars at a local hardware store or such for some sheetmetal etc..and make a nice water blocker to fix your problem.<P>Topknot =]<BR>,||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||05-22-2001||05:14 PM||||Just thought about something. <BR>There is a rubber splash shield that goes in the fender well. Fills the gap between the spaceframe and the engine cradle. Make sure yours is there. If not, water could be splashing in from the wheel well. Right on to the pulleys. <BR>If yours is gone or torn up, check <A HREF="http://www.rodneydickman.com" TARGET=_blank>www.rodneydickman.com</A> <BR>He sells reproductions, and a lot of other nice stuff.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 05-22-2001).]||||reg||1
